1250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048716 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "b815b17a-9466-55a5-83df-3be854054c9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048985 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186101017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048985 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186101018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048985 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186101019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048985 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186101020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048985 #edb/ref 1031 "Optimization Problems in Economics" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048985 #edb/ref 1033 1218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186048985 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "d10de7de-f23e-5cf3-b20d-fb6542a1ea1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050688 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050688 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050688 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186198405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050688 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429350 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050688 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429351 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050688 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429352 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050688 #edb/ref 1031 "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050688 #edb/ref 1033 4164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050688 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "5f2b42f8-b227-5baa-87a7-72698f765d30" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050751 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050751 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050751 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162003 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050751 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186162004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050751 #edb/ref 1031 "Finding the Mode of a Continuous Random Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050751 #edb/ref 1033 3025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050751 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "63937777-cc36-5732-9734-cc239dfdbe45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050536 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186100815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1031 "Optimizing Distances" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1033 1216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1034 1.447359668376152 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050952 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "74e56fe1-9849-55f7-a81d-e8d8802fb0dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064045 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3736" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064045 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of $f(x) = 2x - x^2$  on the interval $[-1,4].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "69180c3b-7c26-5e5f-8d7a-42aed98431bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064045 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2x-x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 2-2x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n2-2x = 0 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad x=1.\n$$\n\nThe critical points are $x=1$ and the endpoints $x=-1,4$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =2(-1)-(-1)^{2}=-3 \\\\ f(1) & =2(1)-(1)^{2}=1 \\\\ f(4) & =2(4)-(4)^{2}=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(1)=1,$ so the global maximum value is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(4)=-8,$ so the global minimum value is $-8.$\n\n![](images/c2/c277beb3423cc80d3ac2a001ac1063059b645c65fc8cb77978050f3831274b31.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064046 #edb/ref 1069 "e-3737" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064046 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global maximum and minimum values for the function $f(x) = x^4 - 6 x^2$ on the closed interval $[-1, 3].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064046 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a0f47cd2-ea8f-5278-ab67-46ecd65d2f4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064046 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=x^4 - 6x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = 4x^3 - 12x,\n$$\n\nand solving $f'(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}-12x & =0 \\\\ 4x({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ 4x(x+\\sqrt{3})(x-\\sqrt{3}) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x = -\\sqrt 3 \\approx -1.73$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, we ignore this point.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0,\\sqrt{3}$ and the endpoints $x=-1,3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\nFinally, we evaluate $f$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(-1) \t& = (-1)^4 - 6(-1)^2 = -5\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(0) \t\t& = (0)^4 - 6(0)^2 = 0\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(\\sqrt 3) \t& = (\\sqrt 3)^4 - 6(\\sqrt 3)^2 = -9\\[5pt]\n \t\tf(3) \t\t& = (3)^4 - 6(3)^2 = 27 \\\t\t\n \t\t}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the global maximum is $27$ and the global minimum is $-9.$\n\n![](images/74/74ce5114dbc93035b0be078be12149cced66ce881dfbc37768ed9febcdfcba87.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186250116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186288019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186288024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186531603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Global Extrema of a Function on a Given Interval" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064163 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e901ea71-aeab-5e95-939e-2401558aa708" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186290433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186292730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186297405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317502 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186317505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186446079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186535053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1039 "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064164 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186064994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186064995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186064996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1045 "The Candidates Test" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186064994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186064451 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b309af6c-4786-5404-a3b4-0a5fc0d4a4ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064994 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186064995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064994 #edb/ref 1061 12797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064994 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b5f6fdc-d0a8-5c91-a879-4f798a936a36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064994 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064995 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186064996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064995 #edb/ref 1061 12798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064995 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8149edd7-e4fe-579d-804f-4e9bf9911f55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064995 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064996 #edb/ref 1061 12799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064996 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5743e9b2-be38-579c-8843-93e55b3e92f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064996 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065348 #edb/ref 1064 "We know how to use the first derivative test to find the local extrema of a continuous function. But how do we find the global extrema of a continuous function on a closed interval $[a,b]?$\n\nWe apply the **candidates test**, which consists of the following three steps.\n\n**Step 1**:  Find all of the critical points. The critical points are our \"candidates.\"\n\n- If the function is defined on a closed interval $[a,b],$ then we must remember to include the endpoints as well.\n\n- If the function is defined on an open interval $(a,b),$ then we do not include the endpoints.\n\n**Step 2**: Determine the value of the function at each of the critical points.\n\n**Step 3**: Compare all the values of $f$ found in step $2.$ The greatest of these values is the global maximum value, and the least is the global minimum value." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065348 #edb/ref 1065 2667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065348 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "e714b234-f163-5ae5-a064-6bef2d3b9fc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065348 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288024 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451032 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186288024 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451033 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186288024 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451034 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186288024 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20976" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288024 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288024 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+8{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-24x+5,$ defined on the closed interval $-2\\le x\\le 2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The global maximum value is $45$\n- **II.** The global minimum value is $-14$\n- **III.** The global maximum value is $18$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186288024 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e9580a2-179f-5d46-9d50-b4d9da5c57d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186288024 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=3{x}^{4}+8{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-24x+5,$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=12{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-12x-24,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-12x-24 & =0 \\\\ 12({x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x-2) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+2)-(x+2) & =0 \\\\ (x+2)({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,-1,\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-2,\\,-1,\\,1,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =3(-2)^{4}+8(-2)^{3}-6(-2)^{2}-24(-2)+5=13 \\\\ f(-1) & =3(-1)^{4}+8(-1)^{3}-6(-1)^{2}-24(-1)+5=18 \\\\ f(1) & =3(1)^{4}+8(1)^{3}-6(1)^{2}-24(1)+5=-14 \\\\ f(2) & =3(2)^{4}+8(2)^{3}-6(2)^{2}-24(2)+5=45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(2)=45,$ so the global maximum is $45.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=-14,$ so the global minimum is $-14.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"\n\n![](images/9b/9b48417439d0e018250328b92561732dd9f4c8bbec157daf33287b20dac7c41f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290427 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451115 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186290427 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451116 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186290427 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186451117 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186290427 #edb/ref 1069 "q-22568" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290427 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290427 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x},$ defined on the interval $[\\frac{1}{2},2],$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The global maximum value is $\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}$\n- **II.** The global minimum value is $2\\sqrt{e}$\n- **III.** The global minimum value is $4\\sqrt[4]{e}$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186290427 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d406ba45-2233-5b01-8129-dbf69421606e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290427 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{e}^{x}\\cdot x-{e}^{x}\\cdot 1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(x-1)}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}(x-1)}{{x}^{2}}=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=1,$ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{1}{2},2$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{{e}^{1/2}}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2\\sqrt{e}≈3.3 \\\\ f(1) & =\\frac{{e}^{1}}{1}=e≈2.7 \\\\ f(2) & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}≈3.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(2)=\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2},$ so the global maximum is $\\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(1)=e,$ so the global minimum is $e.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n![](images/98/98156f05b9b6c71c7e5a5753d024d0851a0a779741545ba2d62d3d63af41e824.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290735 #edb/ref 1080 "-7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186290735 #edb/re