34879 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534879 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534879 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534879 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4fb4d622-2cfe-5123-b12c-76f30da29551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534886 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534886 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534886 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534886 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534641 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534886 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5ead7aa1-dd1c-551b-9401-863495d3e597" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534937 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186534354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534937 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534937 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534937 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186534354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534937 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b88e7600-2c1a-57b5-9965-7ce4ab955ff3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535032 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535032 #edb/ref 1069 "q-20953" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535032 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535032 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1$ on the interval $[-2,\\frac{1}{2}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535032 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7df7ee25-2f51-5474-8055-7ad2776c92c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535032 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}+1,$ we get\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}+6x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}^{2}+6x & =0 \\\\ 6x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=-1,0,$ and the endpoints $x=-2,\\frac{1}{2}$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =2(-2)^{3}+3(-2)^{2}+1=-3 \\\\ f(-1) & =2(-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2}+1=2 \\\\ f(0) & =2(0)^{3}+3(0)^{2}+1=1 \\\\ f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =2{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}+3{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(-1)=f(\\frac{1}{2})=2,$ so the global maximum is $2.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-2)=-3,$ so the global minimum is $-3.$\n\n![](images/b7/b7741f6f67f6070d6050ebaf792598593f98a406bdab91f73356e2c27085b7a0.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535033 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534886 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535033 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344385" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535033 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535033 #edb/ref 1071 "The exact global minimum value of the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4}$ on the closed interval $-3\\le x\\le 5$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535033 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e7a47fb-69a2-506b-bf8c-6f05366124e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535033 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x+4},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+4}}.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all real values of $x$ in the interval. So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=-3,5$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =\\sqrt{-3+4}=\\sqrt{1}=1 \\\\ f(5) & =\\sqrt{5+4}=\\sqrt{9}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(5)=3,$ so the global maximum is $3.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(-3)=1,$ so the global minimum is $1.$\n\n![](images/a6/a638683e712156f829a3d6562de44c6c72b7fb6d2c360fa293b3dbaea7297ea9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535053 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535053 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2750" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535053 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535053 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global minimum value of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ on the closed interval $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535053 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa173a50-134e-5fe9-8151-0ed518869f1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535053 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer. We discard all solutions except $x=\\pi ,$ since it is the only value that belongs to the given interval.\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=\\pi $ and the endpoints $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=1 \\\\ f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(\\pi )=1,$ so the global maximum is $1.$\n\n- The least value is $f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=0,$ so the global minimum is $0.$\n\n![](images/7e/7e0008d35355afebc392a4bc508bd2cf2de5dc32a108f9f9c030d75a24ece076.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535061 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535061 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344375" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535061 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535061 #edb/ref 1071 "The global maximum value of the function $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x+1}$ on the closed interval $0\\le x\\le 8$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535061 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8cfbdaa-2d28-5714-8504-0797810897ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535061 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We find the critical points. Taking the derivative of $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x+1},$ we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nWe can see that ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ for all real values of $x$ in the interval. So there is no solution for ${f}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\nSo, the only critical points are the endpoints $x=0,8$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =2\\sqrt{0+1}=2 \\\\ f(8) & =2\\sqrt{8+1}=2\\sqrt{9}=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step $2.$\n\n- The greatest value is $f(8)=6,$ so the global maximum is $6.$\n\n- The smallest value is $f(0)=2,$ so the global minimum is $2.$\n\n![](images/de/def7a0e41c8534710eec8bfa3c0f6795b5fbb20ea09058403046be5bef4594fb.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
